{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"Reconciled the checkpoint rows without running lints or tests.\n\nChanged rows:\n\n- SR-174 `title`\n  - Before: `\"Work-item identity is allocated once and never re-issued\"`\n  - After: `\"Identity is allocated once and never re-issued\"`\n  - Why: the amended requirement covers both trunk-minted work-item identities and lane-reserved registry identities.\n\n- SR-174 `rationale`\n  - Before: the approved SN-025 allocation rationale plus the keep-or-mint cross-reference.\n  - After: same text, plus: `\"It contributes to SN-027 by assigning the registry identities independently authored by parallel lanes from one reservation record, so concurrent lanes cannot receive the same identity.\"`\n  - Why: states how the widened row realizes SN-027 while retaining approved wording.\n\n- IF-101 `data`\n  - Before: the long reservation-record/carrier/landing description naming `fold_ids`.\n  - After: `\"read_watermark, bump_watermark, reserve_ids, fold_watermark, landing_id_refusal, landing_fold_lag over docs/id-watermark and the id carrier ref\"`\n  - Why: correct built symbol, concise interface surface.\n\n- IF-224 `data`\n  - Before: `human_approves_spine(...)` only.\n  - After: also declares `trunk_name(root)` and `parse_wi_id(value, source)`.\n  - Why: trace now consumes those agent-common services.\n\n- LLR-141 `code_symbol`\n  - Before: `.../claimed_work_branch`\n  - After: adds `claimed_specs/SPEC_NAME/ACTIVE_DIR`.\n  - Why: the built tree-ish claim reader is traced to this row.\n\n- LLR-181 `code_symbol`\n  - Before: existing kitlib symbols.\n  - After: adds `ArchiveError/write_blobs/_listed_blobs`.\n  - Why: actual object-id-preserving tree extraction helpers.\n\n- LLR-337 `code_symbol`\n  - Before: existing plan-gate symbols.\n  - After: adds `lanes_containing`.\n  - Why: `kitlib.git.lanes_containing` is tagged `Implements: SR-154, LLR-337` and is consumed by the squash-claim flow.\n\n- LLR-362 `code_symbol`\n  - Before: included landing-table symbols such as `landing_lines`, `ids_at`, and `landing_refusal`.\n  - After: carrier/allocation symbols only, including `MARK_LINE`, `carried`, reservation parsing/writing, record history, and tracer writer helpers.\n  - Why: separates allocation authority from landing admission.\n\n- LLR-363 `module`\n  - Before: trace, integrate, check, hook.\n  - After: adds `project-trajectory/scripts/id_carrier.py`.\n  - Why: the landing judgment lives partly there.\n\n- LLR-363 `code_symbol`\n  - Before: trace/integrate/check landing entry points.\n  - After: adds the id-carrier landing-table symbols, tree sweep helpers, and settled route symbols.\n  - Why: binds the full landing implementation to the landing LLR.\n\n- LLR-363 `detail`\n  - Before: reservation/claim/carrier/landing-only-record rules and fold behavior.\n  - After: also states retirement-record refusal and the merge-base/staged-tree definitions of an added identity.\n  - Why: dispute sitting 008’s accepted FIX.\n\n- TC-158 `evidence`\n  - Before: existing watermark record tests.\n  - After: adds `test_an_unchanged_committed_baseline_passes_the_floor` and `test_the_live_id_finding_points_a_lane_at_the_reserve_verb`.\n  - Why: covers the committed-baseline control and reserve guidance.\n\n- TC-401 `evidence`\n  - Before: initial reservation/fold cases.\n  - After: adds `test_a_reservation_in_a_lane_behind_trunk_commits_through_the_integrity_floor` and `test_the_claim_folder_reader_reads_each_lanes_work_items_at_a_revision`.\n  - Why: covers the settled integrity-floor and claim-reader paths.\n\n- TC-402 `method` / `expected`\n  - Before: landing table, stale-base, fold, and watermark-conflict coverage.\n  - After: adds code-only-after-trunk-retirement and reserved-then-retired-and-re-authored behavior.\n  - Why: matches the final landing semantics.\n\n- TC-402 `evidence`\n  - Before: eight high-level landing/fold tests.\n  - After: retains them and adds every confirmed refusal, unreadable-claim, own-record, export-ignore, retirement, hook, stale-base, conflict, and dispatcher-refresh test named by change-list items 1, 16, 20, 27, and 35.\n  - Why: evidence now directly exercises every promised refusal arm.\n\nBuilder change-list reconciliation:\n\n- Confirmed: items 1–6, 8, 10–16, 20–23, 27–35, and 36.\n- Refuted/no registry change: item 9 (internal `git_bytes` argument), 17 (fixture-only updates), 18 (LLR-363 now explicitly scopes landing-only-record behavior to added identities), 19 (coordinator-text tests outside this component spine), 24–26, 32’s withdrawn `extract_paths`, and 37’s IF-257 change; their current rows remain true.\n- LLR-337 item 15 was confirmed and amended.\n\nReview findings:\n\n- 001-REVIEW-A F2: done — IF-101 `data`, LLR-362/363 trace maps.\n- 001-REVIEW-A F4: done — TC-402 `evidence`.\n- 001-REVIEW-A F5: done — LLR-363 detail and retirement evidence.\n- 002-REVIEW-B F3: done — IF-101 uses `fold_watermark`.\n- 002-REVIEW-B F4: done — LLR-363 module/symbol map and TC-402 evidence.\n- 003-REVIEW-A F2: done — IF-101 concise data.\n- 003-REVIEW-A F3: done — LLR-362/363 symbol ownership.\n- 003-REVIEW-A F6: done — TC-402 refusal evidence.\n- 004-REVIEW-B F3: done — TC-402 refusal/claim/own-record evidence.\n- 004-REVIEW-B F4: done — LLR-363 landing trace ownership.\n- 004-REVIEW-B F5: done — LLR-363 retirement rule.\n- 005-REVIEW-A F2: done — IF-101 short interface data.\n- 005-REVIEW-A F3: done — LLR-363 detail and TC-402 expected/evidence.\n- 006-REVIEW-B F3: done — TC-402 refusal-driving tests.\n- 006-REVIEW-B F4: done — all slot/squash landing-table evidence.\n- 009-REVIEW-A F2: done — SR-174 title and rationale.\n- 009-REVIEW-A F3: done — TC-402 evidence.\n- 010-REVIEW-B F3: done — TC-402 evidence.\n- 010-REVIEW-B F4: done — LLR-362/363 ownership split.\n- 010-REVIEW-B F5: done — SR-174 title/rationale.\n\nBack-link finding to report, not edit:\n\n- `project-trajectory/scripts/id_carrier.py` functions `landing_lines`, `landing_refusal`, `_read_landing`, `_added_ids`, `_outside_lines`, `_retired_lines`, `ids_at`, and `rev_fold_lag` still carry `Implements: SR-174, LLR-362`.\n- They should carry `Implements: SR-174, LLR-363`, because LLR-363 now owns the landing table and lists them.\n\nArms map:\n\n- SR-174 → LLR-362 (carrier/reservation authority) → TC-401 and TC-158; IF-101.\n- SR-174 → LLR-363 (landing admission/fold/retirement refusal) → TC-402; IF-101.\n- TC-158 verifies SR-174 and IF-101’s watermark-history behavior.\n- TC-401 verifies SR-174, LLR-362, IF-101.\n- TC-402 verifies SR-174, LLR-363, IF-101.\n- IF-101 is cited by TC-158, TC-401, and TC-402.\n- IF-224 supplies trace’s declared trunk and WI parser; IF-257 remains accurate without amendment.\n\nDecomposition hats:\n\n- INTEGRITY-RECOVERABILITY: SR-174, LLR-362, LLR-363.\n- MAINTAINER: SR-174 rationale, LLR-362 rationale.\n- TEST-ENGINEER: TC-158, TC-401, TC-402.\n- CONSISTENCY: IF-101, IF-224, LLR-141, LLR-181, LLR-337.\n- SECURITY, FIRST-RUN-ADOPTER, UNATTENDED-OPS, CROSS-PLATFORM, UX-DESIGNER, UX-ENGINEER, ACCESSIBILITY, PERFORMANCE, PRODUCT-FITNESS: no additional row; no distinct failure class beyond the allocation, durable-state, evidence, or interface-contract obligations already covered.\n\nNo strict lint or pytest command was run this turn, as directed."}}
